      "code_sha256_lf": "e415bba26f6368c9214a3a38fe828bd417a8c53a126f93e5e3a40479e85bf088",
      "rationale": "All three symptoms (format keyword_case, the issue469 psql \\\\copy command, and the CLI --in-place run) trace to the same bug: sqlparse.filters.tokens._CaseFilter.process() inverts its condition (`if not (ttype in self.ttype)`), so it case-converts every token that is NOT the target ttype and leaves the target ttype untouched. For KeywordCaseFilter this means keywords stay lowercase while identifiers and psql \"\\\\command\" tokens get wrongly upper-cased. The expected values come directly from documentation: README.rst's quick-start example shows keyword_case='upper' upper-casing SELECT/FROM while leaving the identifier \"foo\" untouched, docs/source/api.rst states keyword_case \"Changes how keywords are formatted\", the KEYWORDS table marks TO/STDOUT as keywords, and CHANGELOG's issue469 entry (\"Avoid formatting of psql commands\") establishes that Command tokens like \"\\\\copy\" must stay untouched. Each test currently fails because of the inverted condition and will pass once the condition in _CaseFilter.process is corrected to convert only tokens whose ttype matches self.ttype.",
